<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Yusuf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 729–798</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Anṣārī, Abū Yūsuf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Yusuf, born Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Ansari, was a Muslim jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and one of the foremost authorities of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of Islamic law. The leading student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a>, he also studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. The Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> made him Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> (qadi al-qudat), the first time the title had been conferred in Islamic history, with the power to appoint other judges, and through this office Hanafi law spread in the courts of the state. His Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a>, written at the caliph's request, is one of the earliest systematic treatises on taxation and public finance in Islamic law.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Yusuf lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in the 8th century. His genealogy has been traced to Sa'd ibn Habta, a youth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, and his birth date is estimated from the date of his death, around 729. Anecdotes say he was raised poor but with a great appetite for knowledge. His mother wanted him to learn a trade, tailoring according to some sources, and stories suggest he did so while continuing his studies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a> recognised his talent and became his mentor. He studied religious law and traditions in Kufa and Medina under Abu Hanifa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a>, al-Layth ibn Sa'd and others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abu Yusuf lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> until he was appointed qadi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It is unclear whether al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, al-Hadi or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> appointed him. One story says he gave sound advice to a government official, who rewarded him and recommended him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Harun al-Rashid drew him into his inner circle and granted him the title of Grand Qadi, which had never before been conferred in Islamic history. It began as an honorific, but the caliph consulted him on legal matters and financial policy and gave him the ability to appoint other qadis, which made the post like that of a modern chief justice. He held it until his death in 798.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ibn al-Nadim's Kitab al-Fihrist of the 10th century lists many titles by Abu Yusuf, but only the Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a>, a treatise on taxation written at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s request, survives from that list. Others that survive include the Kitab al-Athar, a collection of Kufian traditions, a comparison of the opinions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a> and Ibn Abi Layla, and a refutation of the Syrian scholar al-Awza'i on the laws of war. Excerpts from lost works were preserved by his disciples, among them Muhammad al-Shaybani.</p><p>In the Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a> he advised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> on conducting financial policy in accordance with religious law. The caliph took some suggestions and ignored others, but the overall effect was to limit the ruler's discretion over the tax system. He favoured a proportional tax on produce over fixed taxes on property as an incentive to bring more land into cultivation, and a centralised tax administration to reduce corruption. His main legacy was affirming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> law as the predominant legal thought of the empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Yusuf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=773" aria-label="Kufa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5693 998 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Yusuf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6143 1268h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6143 1268h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=773">What was here in 773 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
